<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Bit Zamani</h1><p class="st-meta">950 BCE – 830 BCE (approx.) · capital Amedi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Bit Zamani, an Aramaean kingdom on the upper Tigris around Amedi, modern Diyarbakır, absorbed by Assyria in the 9th century BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Aramaean groups established Bit Zamani on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse">Late Bronze Age collapse</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadiya">Amedi</a>, the city that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyarbakir">Diyarbakır</a>. It appears in Assyrian sources from the late 10th century BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its rulers controlled the routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Ammi-ba'al made a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurnasirpal-ii">Ashurnasirpal II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> but was murdered by rebels in 879 BCE, prompting an Assyrian campaign.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Assyrian kings reduced Bit Zamani to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> and then, by the late 9th century BCE, a province called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadiya">Amedi</a>. The city has remained a regional centre ever since.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bit-zamani.jpg" alt="Bit Zamani" width="320" height="245"><figcaption>Image: Near_East_topographic_map-blank.svg: Sémhur derivative work: Zunkir (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neo-hittites_et_arameens.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>950 BCE – 830 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Amedi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ammi-ba'al</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Aramaean religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aramaic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Amedi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-890&amp;focus=bit-zamani" aria-label="Bit Zamani on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5769 842 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bit Zamani lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6089 1098L6092 1134"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6092 1134h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6089 1098h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6089 1098h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Amedi. <a href="/atlas?year=-890&amp;focus=bit-zamani">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

